      "text": "From the given information, let's denote Jane's current age as \\( x \\).\n\nAccording to the problem, one year ago, Jane's age was a perfect square. Therefore, we have:\n\\[ x - 1 = n^2 \\quad \\text{(Equation 1)} \\]\nwhere \\( n \\) is an integer.\n\nAdditionally, one year from now, Jane's age will be a perfect cube. Therefore, we have:\n\\[ x + 1 = m^3 \\quad \\text{(Equation 2)} \\]\nwhere \\( m \\) is an integer.\n\nWe are also given that \\( x \\) must be such that there exists an integer \\( n \\) satisfying Equation 1 and an integer \\( m \\) satisfying Equation 2.\n\nLet's first rearrange Equation 1 to solve for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x = n^2 + 1 \\]\n\nSubstituting \\( x \\) into Equation 2, we get:\n\\[ n^2 + 1 + 1 = m^3 \\]\n\\[ n^2 + 2 = m^3 \\]\n\nTo find Jane's age, we need \\( x \\) such that both \\( x - 1 = n^2 \\) and \\( x + 1 = m^3 \\) are integers. We need to find \\( x \\) such that \\( n \\) and \\( m \\) are integers.\n\nLet's test some small values of \\( n \\):\n\n1. If \\( n = 4 \\), then \\( n^2 = 16 \\), so:\n\\[ x = 16 + 1 = 17 \\]\n\nChecking if \\( x + 1 \\) is a perfect cube:\n\\[ 17 + 1 = 18 \\]\n\\[ 18 \\] is not a perfect cube.\n\n2. If \\( n = 9 \\), then \\( n^2 = 81 \\), so:\n\\[ x = 81 + 1 = 82 \\]\n\nChecking if \\( x + 1 \\) is a perfect cube:\n\\[ 82 + 1 = 83 \\]\n\\[ 83 \\] is not a perfect cube.\n\n3. If \\( n = 16 \\), then \\( n^2 = 256 \\), so:\n\\[ x = 256 + 1 = 257 \\]\n\nChecking if \\( x + 1 \\) is a perfect cube:\n\\[ 257 + 1 = 258 \\]\n\\[ 258 \\] is not a perfect cube.\n\n4. If \\( n = 25 \\), then \\( n^2 = 625 \\), so:\n\\[ x = 625 + 1 = 626 \\]\n\nChecking if \\( x + 1 \\) is a perfect cube:\n\\[ 626 + 1 = 627 \\]\n\\[ 627 \\] is not a perfect cube.\n\n5. If \\( n = 49 \\), then \\( n^2 = 2401 \\), so:\n\\[ x = 2401 + 1 = 2402 \\]\n\nChecking if \\( x + 1 \\) is a perfect cube:\n\\[ 2402 + 1 = 2403 \\]\n\\[ 2403 \\] is a perfect cube because \\( 2403 = 15^3 \\).\n\nThus, Jane was 2402 years old one 